<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalinga War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">261 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> War was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>'s conquest of Kalinga under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, dated on the site to 261 BCE. Ashoka's own edicts record the great loss of life it caused. His remorse is traditionally linked to his turn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and his policy of rule by dhamma.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalinga-war.jpg" alt="Kalinga War" width="320" height="270" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jagged 85 at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chandragupta_Maurya_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> was an independent kingdom on the east coast, in the present-day state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> and the northern parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>. It was a prosperous region with ports and a skilled navy, and its people had traded offshore as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Its culture blended tribal religions, Śramaṇa traditions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajivika">Ajivika</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanism">Brahmanism</a>, which existed side by side. The region had been under the Nanda Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> until the fall of the Nanda Empire in 321 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> had possibly tried to conquer it and been repulsed.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> set out to annex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> once he was securely established as emperor. Some scholars argue that Kalinga was a strategic threat to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a>, since it could interrupt communications between the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> and the possessions in the central Indian peninsula, and it also controlled the coastline for trade in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s own edicts, the war was completed in the eighth year of his reign, probably in 261 BCE. It was the only major war he fought after his accession. The Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a>, at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>, reported that the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> had a powerful army of infantry, cavalry and elephants. According to the archaeologist Dilip K. Chakrabarti, the Mauryan army marched to Kalinga through Sarguja, a route <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a> later followed in his own campaign against Kalinga. It is presumed that the battle was fought on the Dhauli hills, on the banks of the Daya River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> recorded the result himself in Rock Edict 13. It states that the Kalingans were conquered eight years after his coronation, that one hundred and fifty thousand were deported, that one hundred thousand were killed and that many more died from other causes. The edict says that he came to feel a strong inclination towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> and deep remorse for having conquered the Kalingans.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, already a Buddhist who was not actively engaged in the faith, devoted the rest of his life to ahimsa (non-violence) and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>-vijaya, victory through dharma. The conquest ended the military expansion of the empire that had begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>, and an era of more than 40 years of relative peace followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kalinga War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Kalinga War</b>, Ashoka's conquest of Kalinga around 261 BCE, which by his own account caused a hundred thousand deaths and the deportation of many more, was the empire's last major war and led him to renounce conquest by force.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
